   "source": [
    "expt.setup_bathymetry(\n",
    "    bathymetry_path='/g/data/ik11/inputs/GEBCO_2022/GEBCO_2022.nc',\n",
    "    longitude_coordinate_name='lon',\n",
    "    latitude_coordinate_name='lat',\n",
    "    vertical_coordinate_name='elevation',\n",
    "    )"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now if you look in the `input_dir`, you'll see a `bathymetry.nc` file. There's also an intermediate file called `bathymetry_raw.nc`. This is a file that's been cut out but not yet regridded - important if you need to do your regridding as a separate job with extra resources, but don't worry about that for now. \n",
    "\n",
    "Just like with the grids above, we can directly plot the bathymetry now:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# To add a land mask to the domain, it's easy to just use the .where method to only plot the points where the bathymetry is not zero\n",
    "expt.bathymetry.depth.where(expt.bathymetry.depth != 0).plot()\n",
    "plt.title(\"Bathymetry of our domain\"), plt.xlabel(\"Longitude\") , plt.ylabel(\"Latitude\");"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Next, we pass on the paths to the initial and boundary conditions. These are the GLORYS files that we downloaded earlier to our `tmp_dir`. \n",
    "\n",
    "Importantly, if you didn't want to force with GLORYS data, you'll need to modify the`ocean_varnames` dictionary that maps the MOM6 variable names (`yh`, `xh` etc.) to the variable names in the dataset. \n",
    "\n",
    "The last thing you need to do is specify which Arakawa grid your input data is on. In the case of the GLORYS dataset, tracers and velocities are on the same grid points, so it's an \"A\" grid. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Applying Arakawa A grid variable mapping, which is velocities and tracers on the same grid\n",
      "Setting up Initial Conditions\n",
      "Regridding Velocities... Done.\n",
      "Regridding Tracers... Done.\n",
      "Regridding Free surface... Done.\n",
      "Saving outputs... Processing north boundary velocity & tracers...Applying Arakawa A grid variable mapping, which is velocities and tracers on the same grid\n",
      "Done.\n",
      "Processing south boundary velocity & tracers...Applying Arakawa A grid variable mapping, which is velocities and tracers on the same grid\n",
      "Done.\n",
      "Processing east boundary velocity & tracers...Applying Arakawa A grid variable mapping, which is velocities and tracers on the same grid\n",
      "Done.\n",
      "Processing west boundary velocity & tracers...Applying Arakawa A grid variable mapping, which is velocities and tracers on the same grid\n",
      "Done.\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Mapping dictionary between MOM6 variable names (left) and those in GLORYS (right), which is the source dataset for the forcing files\n",
    "# If you use a different source dataset, simply replace the GLORYS names with those in your dataset of choice\n",
    "\n",
    "ocean_varnames = {\"time\": \"time\",\n",
    "                  \"yh\": \"latitude\", \n",
    "                  \"xh\": \"longitude\",\n",
    "                  \"zl\": \"depth\",\n",
    "                  \"eta\": \"zos\",\n",
    "                  \"u\": \"uo\",\n",
    "                  \"v\": \"vo\",\n",
    "                  \"tracers\": {\"salt\": \"so\", \"temp\": \"thetao\"} # You can have as many tracers here as you like, and they will all get regridded to your ICs / BCs\n",
    "                  }\n",
    "\n",
    "# Set up the initial condition.\n",
    "expt.setup_initial_condition(\n",
    "    tmp_dir + \"/ic_unprocessed.nc\",\n",
    "    ocean_varnames,\n",
    "    arakawa_grid = \"A\"\n",
    "    )\n",
    "\n",
    "# Set up the four boundary conditions.\n",
    "expt.setup_ocean_state_boundaries(\n",
    "    Path(tmp_dir),\n",
    "    ocean_varnames,\n",
    "    arakawa_grid = \"A\"\n",
    "    )"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now we've made our initial and boundary conditions, these files are also now in our `input_dir`. The initial conditions can also be plotted via our experiment object. Remember that calling `expt.init_tracers` just returns an xarray object, so everything from there on is just xarray syntax"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#fig,ax = plt.subplots(1,3,figsize=(20, 6))\n",
    "# Plot the surface values of temperature, salinity and sea surface height for the initial conditions\n",
    "#expt.init_tracers.temp.isel(zl = 0).where(expt.bathymetry.depth != 0).plot(ax=ax[0],cmap = \"plasma\")\n",
    "#expt.init_tracers.salt.isel(zl = 0).where(expt.bathymetry.depth != 0).plot(ax=ax[1])\n",
    "#expt.init_eta.eta_t.where(expt.bathymetry.depth != 0).plot(ax=ax[2],cmap = \"RdBu\")\n",
    "#ax[0].set_title(\"Initial temperature\"), ax[1].set_title(\"Initial salinity\"), ax[2].set_title(\"Initial sea surface height\")\n",
    "#for i in ax:\n",
    "#    i.set_xlabel(\"Longitude\"), i.set_ylabel(\"Latitude\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "5. Set up the surface forcing\n",
    "\n",
    "The last step is to set up the data atmosphere to use the ERA5 data product. Since the raw ERA5 data isn't in a suitable format to force an ocean model, `regional-mom6` pre-processes the raw data to create a data forcing product for your experiment. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "expt.setup_era5(\"/g/data/rt52/era5/single-levels/reanalysis/\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Set up the run directory\n",
    "\n",
    "So far, each of the methods we've run have populated the `input_dir` with data files, like the grids, bathymetry, forcing files etc. The last step is to set up the run directory with the text files that the model needs. These include the `diag_table`, `MOM_input` and `MOM_override` files for MOM6, the `nuopc.runconfig` for the nuopc coupler, and the `config.yaml` file for payu. \n",
    "\n",
    "This final step uses all of the information that `regional-mom6` knows about your experiment to write out these files, creating a run directory that's ready to be run with the `payu` scheduler. \n",
    "\n",
    "First, we need to copy the template files from `ACCESS-NRI`, which are kept up to date with their MOM6 builds.\n",
    "\n",
    "Navigate to your run directory (specified above) and run the following"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "`git clone https://github.com/ACCESS-NRI/access-om3-configs.git -b M_regional_template`"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Then, running the following cell will build on this template to set up your model run. Note that this will work even if you change your domain from the Tasmania example - `regional-mom6` makes all the necessary modifications, it just needs initial template files to build on.\n",
    "\n",
    "**IMPORTANT**\n",
    "The `overwrite` flag means business. If you set `overwrite=True`, then `regional-mom6` will delete everything in your run directory and re-clone from github. This doesn't affect files in your input directory (all the `.nc` files) but _will_ undo any manual edits you've made in your run directory. With `overwrite=False`, `regional-mom6` instead attempts to modify existing files."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 7,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Overwrite set to True. Run directory /g/data/tm70/hm6113/rom3-rundirs/access-rom3-demo to be emptied and replaced with\n",
      "a fresh template from OM3-configs branch 1463-M_regional_template\n",
      "Cloning with\n",
      "git clone --branch 1463-M_regional_template --single-branch https://github.com/ACCESS-NRI/access-om3-configs /g/data/tm70/hm6113/rom3-rundirs/access-rom3-demo --depth 1\n",
      "ERA5 set to True - attempting to create an ESMF mesh for the surface forcing using\n",
      "python3 /g/data/vk83/apps/om3-scripts/mesh_generation/generate_mesh.py --grid-type=latlon --grid-filename=/g/data/tm70/hm6113/rom3-inputdirs/access-rom3-demo/10u_ERA5.nc --mesh-filename=/g/data/tm70/hm6113/rom3-inputdirs/access-rom3-demo/era5-ESMF-mesh.nc --wrap-lons=True\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "name": "stderr",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "/g/data/vk83/apps/om3-scripts/mesh_generation/generate_mesh.py:468: UserWarning: Cannot automatically determine the name of the area variable. If area is available in the input grid file and it needs to be included in the mesh, please pass --area-name\n",
      "  warnings.warn(\n",
      "/g/data/vk83/apps/om3-scripts/scripts_common.py:133: UserWarning: /g/data/vk83/apps/om3-scripts/mesh_generation/generate_mesh.py not under git version control! Add your file to a repository before generating any production output.\n",
      "  warn(\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "expt.setup_rOM3(branch = \"1463-M_regional_template\",overwrite=True)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Run with payu\n",
    "\n",
    "This step takes place in a terminal on gadi inside your run directory. For further information about the `payu` workflow management tool, see the [documentation](https://payu.readthedocs.io/en/latest/)\n",
    "\n",
    "1. load payu with\n",
    "\n",
    "    `module use /g/data/vk83/modules; module load payu`\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Set up the experiment:\n",
    "\n",
    "    `payu setup`\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Run the experiment\n",
    "\n",
    "    `payu run -f`\n"
   ]
  }
 ],
